{"candidates":[{"claim":"A coin system C is canonical if and only if greedy uses the minimum coins for all amounts w < c_{n-1} + c_n. Thus a pseudo-polynomial DP up to c_{n-1} + c_n decides canonicity in O(n * (c_{n-1} + c_n)) time.","counterconditions":["If the simulation finds a coin system where the smallest counterexample exceeds c_{n-1} + c_n, this claim is refuted.","If the bound is correct but Pearson's algorithm provides a polynomial bound, this pseudo-polynomial algorithm is suboptimal."],"mechanism":"By Kozen and Zaks, if a counterexample exists, the smallest one is strictly bounded by c_{n-1} + c_n. The algorithm computes OPT(w) via DP and G(w) via greedy for all w up to c_{n-1} + c_n. If G(w) > OPT(w) for some w, it returns the smallest such w; otherwise returns canonical.","optional_refs":["SRC_001"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["SRC_001"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"claim":"There exists a decision procedure for coin canonicity that is strictly polynomial in the number of coins n, specifically running in O(n^3) time, and it finds the smallest counterexample if one exists.","counterconditions":["If no such polynomial algorithm exists (e.g., the problem is NP-hard), this claim is refuted.","If the O(n^3) algorithm fails to find the smallest counterexample on a tested system, it is refuted."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Pearson's algorithm (2005) constructs specific candidate amounts based on residues modulo the largest coin and checks the canonicity condition in O(n^3) by testing a finite candidate set of size O(n^2). The algorithm checks the 'one-coin' and 'multiple-coin' test bounds directly.","optional_refs":["SRC_001"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["SRC_001"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"claim":"A coin system C is canonical if and only if it is canonical for all amounts w < c_{n-2} + c_{n-1} + c_n. A pseudo-polynomial DP up to this bound provides a safe (but potentially loose) decision procedure in O(n * (c_1)) time.","counterconditions":["If the simulation finds a system where the smallest counterexample exceeds c_{n-2} + c_{n-1} + c_n, this claim is refuted."],"mechanism":"As a fallback bound, if the Kozen-Zaks bound of c_{n-1} + c_n is violated, a sum of the three smallest coins might provide a correct upper bound for the smallest counterexample. We compute OPT and G up to this limit and return the smallest discrepancy.","optional_refs":["SRC_001"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["SRC_001"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.3}],"research_proposals":[{"purpose":"Retrieve details on Pearson's polynomial-time algorithm for deciding canonicity of coin systems, and any known bounds on the smallest counterexample (e.g., Kozen-Zaks).","request_identifier":"res_change_making_001","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Change-making_problem"]}],"simulation_proposals":[{"declared_assumptions":["For coin systems with largest coin up to 12, searching amounts up to 4 * max_c is sufficient to find the smallest counterexample if it exists."],"discriminating_purpose":"Test the candidate bound W(C) = c_{n-1} + c_n against W(C) = c_{n-2} + c_{n-1} + c_n by enumerating small coin systems, finding the true smallest counterexample by brute force, and counting how many systems violate each bound.","hypothesis":"The bound c_{n-1} + c_n will be violated by at least one enumerated coin system, whereas c_{n-2} + c_{n-1} + c_n will not be violated.","interpretation_conditions":["If violators_c2_plus_c1 > 0, the bound c_{n-1} + c_n is refuted.","If violators_c3_plus_c2_plus_c1 > 0, the bound c_{n-2} + c_{n-1} + c_n is refuted."],"model_source":"def simulate(inputs, rng):\n    max_c = inputs[\"max_c\"]\n    from itertools import combinations\n    violators_c2_plus_c1 = 0\n    violators_c3_plus_c2_plus_c1 = 0\n    max_counterexample = 0\n    total_systems = 0\n    def opt_count(w, coins):\n        dp = [0] * (w + 1)\n        for i in range(1, w + 1):\n            dp[i] = min(dp[i - c] + 1 for c in coins if c <= i)\n        return dp[w]\n    def greedy_count(w, coins):\n        count = 0\n        for c in coins:\n            count += w // c\n            w %= c\n        return count\n    for n in range(2, 5):\n        for rest in combinations(range(2, max_c + 1), n - 1):\n            coins = sorted(list(rest) + [1], reverse=True)\n            total_systems += 1\n            bound_1 = coins[-2] + coins[-1]\n            bound_2 = sum(coins[-3:])\n            search_up_to = max_c * 4\n            found = -1\n            for w in range(1, search_up_to):\n                if greedy_count(w, coins) > opt_count(w, coins):\n                    found = w\n                    break\n            if found != -1:\n                if found > bound_1:\n                    violators_c2_plus_c1 += 1\n                if found > bound_2:\n                    violators_c3_plus_c2_plus_c1 += 1\n                if found > max_counterexample:\n                    max_counterexample = found\n    return {\n        \"violators_c2_plus_c1\": violators_c2_plus_c1,\n        \"violators_c3_plus_c2_plus_c1\": violators_c3_plus_c2_plus_c1,\n        \"max_counterexample\": max_counterexample,\n        \"total_systems\": total_systems\n    }","parameter_definitions":[{"name":"max_c","values_json":"[12]"}],"request_identifier":"sim_bound_test_001","requested_observables":["violators_c2_plus_c1","violators_c3_plus_c2_plus_c1","max_counterexample","total_systems"],"requested_seed_set":[1],"rival_predictions":["violators_c2_plus_c1 == 0","violators_c2_plus_c1 > 0"],"simulation_mode":"sandboxed_python_v1"}]}